 abstract=f'''A monitor's warning can be counted as a false alarm when final database state alone defines success. We audit how declared targets change negative classes and warning timing. Three separately analyzed cohorts cover 60 reserved retail and airline test IDs, with {tot['completed']} completed conversations and {len(r['incomplete'])} retained interrupted attempts. In the original cohort, all 39 state successes violate an explicit interaction constraint, and Gemini's first warning coincides with that violation in all 48 conversations. Additional monitor configurations do not necessarily share this timing. The final expansion supplies {r['clean_successes']} interaction-clean state successes across {r['clean_task_ids']} tasks; Gemini, DeepSeek and GPT-5.4 Mini alert on {count('gemini')}, {count('deepseek')} and {count('gptmini')}, respectively. These are proxy-relative alert rates, not full-policy error rates. The exact interaction-rule baseline changes from {r['state_successes']-r['clean_successes']}/{r['state_successes']} to 0/{r['clean_successes']} apparent false alarms solely through the negative-class definition. In 24 constructed cancellation pairs, every operation executes although half violate the executable eligibility rule. Both negative-class selection and a focused monitoring target change alert tradeoffs in configuration-dependent directions. We release matched public inputs, transparent baselines, missing-output accounting and executable analyses. The findings concern target alignment in a finite benchmark audit; they establish neither calibrated failure prediction nor comprehensive policy compliance.'''
